Twist NEWS -- history of user-visible changes

Copyright (C) 2026 Chris Burdess

Please send Twist bug reports to <dog@gnu.org>

Version 0.1 - unreleased

  * HTTP/1, HTTP/2, and HTTP/3, plus FTP, mail, MQTT, LDAP,
    WebSocket, Gopher, DICT, Telnet, RTSP, TFTP, and FILE
  * telnet sessions: the server's text is written out, its option
    negotiation is answered, and standard input (or -T) is sent; -t sets the
    terminal type, display, environment and window size
  * rtsp:// as the curl tool does it: one OPTIONS request, the reply written
    as the server sent it, exit status 85 for a CSeq that does not match;
    -I now writes the headers, and -i the blank line before the body, not
    after it
  * the cookie jar (library only) keeps every cookie for the host that set it;
    a Domain attribute widens nothing, so no Public Suffix List is needed
  * -i, -I and -D write an HTTP/1 status line with its reason phrase, as the
    server sent it, and an HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 one as "HTTP/2 200", not the
    pseudo-header ":status"; -w %{http_version}; TWISTINFO_HTTP_VERSION
  * a connection is shared only between transfers with the same TLS settings
    (verification, CA, client certificate and key, an https proxy's, and
    proxy credentials)
  * Mac OS and the BSDs: the event loop no longer fails to watch a descriptor
    that is new to the kernel's queue and wanted for reading only
  * --stderr - is standard output, as curl's is; a binary body (one with a
    NUL) is not written to a terminal unless -o - says so (exit 23, and a
    warning); --no-OPTION for every option that is a switch
  * a response to HEAD, and a 204 or a 304, has no body whatever its headers
    say: -I no longer waits for one (exit 52 on HTTP/1.0, a hang on keep-alive)
  * --connect-timeout covers the greeting and the login of smtp, imap, pop3
    and ftp (and the schemes with an s), as curl's does, and not only the TCP
    connect: a server that is silent, or greets and goes quiet, ends the
    transfer at the limit with status 28
  * a redirect goes by scheme alone: from http to http, https, ftp and ftps,
    from https to https and ftps, and from nothing else (a WebSocket
    handshake included) to anything; an ftp or ftps target is followed, as a
    transfer of the FTP handler that does not take the request's user and
    password; --proto-redir is accepted, warns that it has no effect, and does
    nothing
  * names are looked for in /etc/hosts before any DNS is asked, and localhost
    and any name under it are the loopback (::1 and 127.0.0.1, RFC 6761), as
    they are not known to a public resolver
  * the resolver asks for AAAA, A and HTTPS together, as separate queries in one
    flight, with the resolution delay of RFC 8305, and so a name that has only
    an IPv6 address can be reached
  * a connection to a name with several addresses is raced (Happy Eyeballs,
    RFC 8305): IPv6 first, the families taking turns, the next address tried
    after 250 ms or at once when an attempt fails, and the first to connect
    kept; for http, https, ftp, ftps and the mail and other line protocols
    (not HTTP/3 or a connection through a proxy, which use one address)
  * the file given to -o is made when there is something to put in it, so a
    transfer that fails first leaves none, and does not empty one that was there
  * --limit-rate for every scheme but tftp, kept on the connection (not over
    HTTP/3, which an https transfer then does not use)
  * a parked sftp or scp session that is dropped (idle for two minutes, or at
    exit) says goodbye to the server with an SSH disconnect before the
    connection closes
  * a parked sftp or scp session that the server closed an instant before it
    was taken is not a failed transfer: if nothing has come from the server
    and no upload has been read, the transfer goes again on a new connection
  * an sftp or scp transfer that fails because the file is missing or may not
    be opened keeps its session for the next URL
  * socks4:// and socks4a:// proxies; socks5:// and socks4:// look the name up
    here and give the proxy an address, socks5h:// and socks4a:// give it the
    name
  * a plain http:// URL through an HTTP proxy sends the proxy's credentials
    as Proxy-Authorization
  * a connection says when it holds a chunk that has not been written, and a
    request body of 64 KiB or more for HTTP/1 and the message of smtp are sent
    as the connection writes them, not copied into its queue whole
  * the file given to -T is a glob pattern ({a,b}, [1-9]; -g turns it off): each
    file goes to the URL, and #N in the URL, in -o and in --output-dir is the
    Nth glob, the URL's first and the file's after them; a URL that ends in a
    slash takes the file's name for http and https too
  * a connection that its server closed while it was parked no longer touches
    freed memory when the runtime is freed
  * ftp and ftps upload (-T: STOR; -a/--append: APPE; -C N or -C -: APPE after
    the first N bytes, or the size the server has, and a download resumes with
    REST), and imap APPEND (-T: the message is a literal and its size must be
    known), both sent as the connection takes them and not held in memory; a
    refused upload is status 25
  * smtp and tftp uploads with -T are read as they are sent, a chunk at a time,
    and not held in memory; sftp stops for a connection that has not written
    what it has, and so does what goes through an https proxy's tunnel
  * request bodies over HTTP/2 and HTTP/3, and the message of a WebSocket, are
    sent a chunk at a time as the connection (and the server's windows) can
    take them, and not queued whole; the compressed body of an HTTP/2 request
    (--content-encoding) is no longer freed while it is still being sent
  * -T uploads to http and https are read as they are sent (Content-Length for
    a file, chunked for a pipe) and not held in memory; a redirect or a second
    request that has the data sent again starts a file over, and a pipe fails
    with status 65
  * cookies expire: Expires and Max-Age are read (Max-Age wins; no cookie lasts
    more than 400 days), and an expired cookie or Max-Age=0 deletes
  * gzip and deflate request and response coding, via zlib
  * the progress meter and the bar (-#), and the library's
    TWISTOPT_XFERINFOFUNCTION
  * https proxies (--proxy-cacert, --proxy-insecure): TLS to the proxy and
    CONNECT in HTTP/2 or HTTP/1.1, for every scheme but ftp
  * HTTP/3 through an https proxy, with CONNECT-UDP (RFC 9298), and
    --proxy-template-udp for the proxy's URI Template
  * environment variables: http_proxy, HTTPS_PROXY,
    [scheme]_PROXY, ALL_PROXY, NO_PROXY, COLUMNS, SSL_CERT_FILE, SSL_CERT_DIR,
    SSLKEYLOGFILE, QLOGDIR; with --noproxy and --capath; TWIST_CA_BUNDLE names
    a CA bundle
  * -Z/--parallel runs the URLs of a command line together, up to
    --parallel-max (50 by default, 300 at most), with curl's exit status for
    them; --fail-early ends the transfers under way, and standard input goes
    to one transfer only
  * the progress meter of -Z is one line for the run, in curl's layout for
    parallel transfers, and is drawn on standard error unless -s,
    --no-progress-meter, or a body going to a terminal says not
  * transfers started together to one host share an HTTP/2 or HTTP/3
    connection, over TLS as well as in the clear (TWISTOPT_PIPEWAIT, and
    --parallel-immediate for none), and twist_runtime_setopt with
    TWIST_RUNTIMEOPT_MAX_HOST_CONNECTIONS, or --parallel-max-host with -Z, keeps
    the connections to one host to a number
  * twist_runtime_setopt with TWIST_RUNTIMEOPT_THREADS, and --threads, run
    transfers on several event loops, each in a thread of its own but the
    first; transfers that can share a connection go to one loop, the rest are
    spread, the limit on connections to a host holds across them, and the done
    callback is always called on the thread that drives the runtime
